lasses are available peri-operatively, maintain sleep-wake cycle, avoid benzodiazepines\n\n4. **Medication Review**:\n   - Consider deprescribing omeprazole (prolonged PPI use, low benefit in many elderly patients)\n   - Review gabapentin — may be contributing to fatigue, falls, and cognitive dulling. Consider dose reduction or switch to duloxetine monotherapy for neuropathic pain.\n   - Ensure aspirin management plan with surgeon (typically held 7 days pre-op)\n\n**Measurable Benchmarks for Surgical Readiness** (reassess at 6-8 weeks):\n1. **Gait speed**: Improvement from 0.36 m/s toward >0.6 m/s (below 0.6 m/s is associated with very poor surgical outcomes)\n2. **Grip strength**: Improvement from 16 kg toward >20 kg\n3. **Timed Up and Go (TUG)**: Achieve <20 seconds (currently would likely be >30 seconds)\n4. **Chair stand test**: Ability to perform 5 sit-to-stands from a standard chair without armrests in <15 seconds\n5. **Nutritional markers**: Albumin >3.0, weight gain of >=3 lbs, prealbumin >18\n6. **Depression**: PHQ-9 improvement to <10\n7. **Engagement**: Patient demonstrates ability to participate actively in therapy 3x/week without excessive fatigue or non-compliance\n\n**Decision Framework**:\n- If benchmarks are met: Proceed with TKA with enhanced perioperative care (geriatric co-management, delirium prevention, early mobilization protocol)\n- If benchmarks are partially met: Re-discuss risks and benefits with patient and family. Consider additional prehab time.\n- If benchmarks are not met: Recommend against elective TKA. Continue conservative management with optimized prehab gains as the treatment.\n- **If the patient cannot tolerate prehab**: This is strong evidence that he will not tolerate post-surgical rehabilitation. Surgery should not proceed.',
