ll severely frail. This means his body\'s reserves — strength, nutrition, endurance — are significantly depleted. When we operate on frail patients, the complication rates are much higher, and the functional improvement is often less than we would hope. This is not a reflection of his willpower or your family\'s support — it is a biological reality.\n\nI have designed a prehabilitation program to see if we can improve his fitness enough to make surgery safer. But I want to be transparent — even with prehab, the surgery carries meaningful risk for someone in his condition.\n\nI also want to make sure we are all hearing from the most important person in this room. [Patient\'s name], is there anything you would like to share with your daughter about how you are feeling about all of this?\'\n\n**Key Principles**:\n- **Refused to be the "bad guy"** — this would undermine the patient\'s autonomy and the family relationship\n- **Empowered the patient** to express his own wishes with support\n- **Used the medical evaluation as a bridge** — the objective data creates a natural opening for the patient to express his preference without it feeling like a personal rejection of his daughter\n- **Screened for depression and grief** — his lack of motivation could reflect untreated bereavement/depression, which is treatable and might change his perspective\n- **Did not make the decision for anyone** — facilitated a conversation where the patient, family, and medical team can reach a shared understanding\n- **Maintained professional integrity** — did not misrepresent the medical assessment to serve as a social buffer',
